-- Writing (24 prompt)

You need a meeting room for Thursday at 10am for five people. Write a short message to the office admin. Say the day, the time, and the number of people. (30–50 words)', 'A strong response gives the day, time, and number of people clearly using simple present/future forms.'),
  ('writing', 'A1', 'prompt', 'email', 'Thank You Note

A colleague helped you finish a report yesterday. Write a short note to thank them. Say what they did and why it helped you. (30–50 words)', 'A strong response names the colleague, says what they did, and expresses genuine thanks in simple language.'),
  ('writing', 'A1', 'prompt', 'general', 'Broken Printer

The printer on your floor is not working. Write a short message to IT support. Say where the printer is and what the problem is. (30–50 words)', 'A strong response states the location and the problem clearly using simple vocabulary.'),
  ('writing', 'A1', 'prompt', 'general', 'Asking for the Wifi Password

You are working from a different office today and you do not have the wifi password. Write a short message to a colleague asking for it. (30–50 words)', 'A strong response politely asks for the information and gives a brief reason using simple present forms.'),
  ('writing', 'A2', 'prompt', 'email', 'Rescheduling a Delivery

A delivery is planned for Monday morning but your office is closed that day. Write a short email to the supplier. Explain the problem and suggest a new day. (40–60 words)', 'A strong response explains the problem and proposes a new day clearly and politely.'),
  ('writing', 'A2', 'prompt', 'email', 'Team Lunch Reminder

Your team is having lunch together on Friday at 1pm at a restaurant near the office. Write a short reminder message to the team. Include the day, time, and place. (40–60 words)', 'A strong response includes the day, time, and place using clear, simple sentences.'),
  ('writing', 'A2', 'prompt', 'email', 'Requesting a Supply Order

Your department is running low on paper and pens. Write a short email to the office manager. List what you need and say when you need it by. (40–60 words)', 'A strong response lists the needed items and states a deadline clearly.'),
  ('writing', 'A2', 'prompt', 'email', 'Note to Security About a Visitor

A visitor is arriving tomorrow at 9am to see you. Write a short message to the security desk. Give the visitor''s name, arrival time, and who they are meeting. (40–60 words)', 'A strong response gives the visitor''s name, arrival time, and who they are meeting.'),
  ('writing', 'B1', 'prompt', 'email', 'Requesting Feedback on a Draft

You have finished a first draft of a report and need a colleague''s opinion before sending it to your manager. Write an email asking them to review it. Explain what the report is about, what kind of feedback you need, and by when you need it. (60–90 words)', 'A strong response explains the report''s topic, the kind of feedback needed, and a deadline in clear, organized sentences.'),
  ('writing', 'B1', 'prompt', 'general', 'Temporary Schedule Change

Due to building maintenance, your office will open one hour later than usual next week. Write an announcement to your colleagues. Explain the reason, the new opening time, and how long the change will last. (60–90 words)', 'A strong response explains the reason, the new time, and the duration of the change using clear linking language.'),
  ('writing', 'B1', 'prompt', 'email', 'Change of Contact Person

You are going on leave and a colleague will handle your emails while you are away. Write a message to your regular contacts. Explain when you will be away, who will replace you, and how to reach that person. (60–90 words)', 'A strong response states the dates of absence, names the replacement, and explains how to reach them.'),
  ('writing', 'B1', 'prompt', 'email', 'Following Up on an Unanswered Email

You sent an email a week ago asking a supplier for a price quote, but you have not received a reply. Write a polite follow-up email. Remind them of your original request and ask when you can expect an answer. (60–90 words)', 'A strong response politely reminds the reader of the original request and asks for a response, using appropriate hedging language.'),
  ('writing', 'B2', 'prompt', 'email', 'Introducing a New Team Member to Clients

A new colleague is joining your team and will now handle some of your regular client accounts. Write an email to your main clients introducing this person. Mention their role, their experience, and reassure clients about the quality of service continuing. (80–120 words)', 'A strong response introduces the new colleague, states their role and experience, and reassures the reader with a professional, warm tone.'),
  ('writing', 'B2', 'prompt', 'email', 'Requesting Additional Budget for a Project

Your project needs more funding than originally planned because of unexpected costs. Write an email to your manager requesting extra budget. Explain why the costs increased, how much more you need, and what will happen if the request is not approved. (80–120 words)', 'A strong response explains the reason for the extra cost, states the amount needed, and outlines the consequence of non-approval clearly and diplomatically.'),
  ('writing', 'B2', 'prompt', 'email', 'Addressing a Scheduling Conflict with a Supplier

A supplier has proposed a delivery date that clashes with a company holiday. Write an email explaining the conflict tactfully and proposing two alternative dates that would work for your team. (80–120 words)', 'A strong response tactfully explains the conflict and proposes concrete alternatives using polite, professional language.'),
  ('writing', 'B2', 'prompt', 'email', 'Responding to an Internal Survey with Suggestions

Your company recently ran a staff satisfaction survey, and you were asked to share ideas for improving remote working arrangements. Write an email to HR with two or three concrete suggestions, explaining briefly why each one would help. (80–120 words)', 'A strong response offers specific, actionable suggestions with brief justifications in a professional tone.'),
  ('writing', 'C1', 'prompt', 'email', 'Explaining a Data-Security Incident to Staff

A minor security incident affected some internal files last week, and it has now been resolved. Write an email to all staff explaining what happened, what steps were taken to fix it, and what precautions employees should take going forward, without causing unnecessary alarm. (100–150 words)', 'A strong response explains the incident and the fix calmly, balancing transparency with reassurance, using appropriately formal register.'),
  ('writing', 'C1', 'prompt', 'email', 'Turning Down a Partnership Offer Diplomatically

A smaller company has proposed a business partnership that does not align with your company''s current strategy. Write an email declining the offer. Thank them for their interest, explain your reasoning tactfully, and leave the door open for future opportunities. (100–150 words)', 'A strong response thanks the sender, tactfully explains the misalignment with company strategy, and leaves the relationship open for the future.'),
  ('writing', 'C1', 'prompt', 'email', 'Managing Client Expectations After a Missed Deadline

Your team missed an important deadline for a client due to an internal staffing issue. Write an email to the client acknowledging the delay, taking appropriate responsibility, explaining the cause without making excuses, and outlining a clear revised plan to rebuild their confidence. (100–150 words)', 'A strong response acknowledges the delay, takes appropriate responsibility without over-apologizing, and outlines a credible revised plan.'),
  ('writing', 'C1', 'prompt', 'email', 'Mediating a Disagreement Between Two Departments

The marketing and finance departments disagree about how to allocate budget for an upcoming campaign, and it is affecting progress. Write an email as a project lead proposing a fair compromise, acknowledging both sides'' concerns and suggesting a way forward that keeps the project on track. (100–150 words)', 'A strong response acknowledges both departments'' concerns and proposes a fair, well-reasoned compromise that keeps the project moving.'),
  ('writing', 'C2', 'prompt', 'email', 'Persuading Leadership to Reverse a Decision

Senior management has decided to cancel a project you believe is valuable to the company''s long-term strategy. Write a persuasive email to the leadership team making the case for reconsidering the decision. Use evidence, anticipate likely objections, and maintain a respectful, professional tone throughout. (120–180 words)', 'A strong response explains what happened, the remediation taken, and practical precautions, balancing transparency with reassurance.'),
  ('writing', 'C2', 'prompt', 'general', 'Responding to a Public Review Crisis

Your company has received a wave of negative public reviews after a service failure went viral on social media. Write an official public statement addressing the criticism, acknowledging shortcomings honestly, outlining corrective action, and rebuilding trust with customers, while protecting the company''s reputation. (120–180 words)', 'A strong response persuasively makes the case using evidence, anticipates objections, and closes on partnership rather than one-off assistance.'),
  ('writing', 'C2', 'prompt', 'general', 'Announcing Organizational Changes Sensitively

Due to restructuring, several roles in your department will be eliminated next month. Write an internal announcement to the affected team informing them of the changes with honesty and compassion, explaining the support available to them, and maintaining morale among remaining staff. (120–180 words)', 'A strong response acknowledges the shortcoming honestly, outlines concrete corrective action, and rebuilds trust without sounding defensive.'),
  ('writing', 'C2', 'prompt', 'negotiation', 'Negotiating Extended Payment Terms with a Struggling Client

A long-standing client is facing financial difficulty and has asked to extend their payment terms from 30 to 90 days. Write an email proposing a compromise arrangement that protects your company''s cash flow while showing empathy and preserving the long-term business relationship. (120–180 words)', 'A strong response proposes a compromise that protects cash flow while showing empathy for the client''s situation, using diplomatic, precise language.');

